  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.4 × loss over x, z from -2 to 2. It is steeper along x (across the view), so overshoot shows there first. Steps diverge without noise once learning rate > 0.5.",
    "Each update: step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian per axis with sd 3·√((64−B)/(63·B)) for batch B, so it is 3 at B=1 and exactly 0 at B=64.",
    "Purple arrow: the next mini-batch step, the way the ball will move. Dark arrow: the step the full 64 points would give. Both are drawn at 1.2 × their true length.",
    "Noise values come from a fixed seed and a counter. New run keeps counting, so its noise differs. Reset starts the same sequence again.",
    "A run is finished after 40 steps. It is diverged when the loss goes above 8 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. Path points off the bowl are drawn at its rim, and the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The trail holds the start plus up to 40 landing points, one per step. The ghost holds the kept run the same way. Running makes 5 updates per second at 1× speed."
  ]
}